def p(text,keys=[]):return {'text':text,'source_keys':keys,'citation_spans':[{'after':text,'source_keys':keys}] if keys else [],'citation_omission_reason':'Scope definition or explicit editorial inference; source measurements cited separately.'}
content={
'summary':[
('Banana edible flesh has both detected metals and non-detect results, depending on the survey and analytical sensitivity. FDA found nickel in six of 27 raw-banana composites and total arsenic in one; lead, cadmium, total mercury, chromium and uranium were non-detect at their respective reporting limits. Those bounds describe tested composites, rather than an absence of metals from bananas.',[F]),
('The South Korean survey measured mean lead of 13.5 µg/kg and nickel of 107.2 µg/kg fresh weight in 15 banana samples. Banana had lower mean lead than pineapple in the same study, but higher mean nickel. The useful conclusion is analyte-specific: a fruit that was lower for one metal was not necessarily lower for another, and these overlapping market samples do not establish a universal substitute or a cleanest growing country.',[L])],
'identity':[
('The principal material here is the edible portion of banana fruit. Lee and colleagues washed, peeled and homogenized their fruit before measuring fresh-weight concentrations; Mekonnen and colleagues also separated the edible portion but dried it before analysis. Their results therefore describe different measurement bases even though both began with fresh bananas.',[L,M]),
('Peel, whole-fruit preparations, plantain, banana powder, chips, extracts and mixed infant foods are separate material questions. A raw edible-pulp concentration is not a measured concentration in any of those derivatives. No moisture factor or preparation yield is supplied here to turn the dry analytical sample into fresh pulp or a commercial dried product.',[])],
'occurrence':[
('The findings below retain each source’s food form, statistic, basis and sampling unit. Five rows offer numerical same-study contrasts with another named fruit. FDA results remain a separate composite survey; a detected-only range excludes the non-detects, and a below-bound mean in the Korean paper does not mean every sample was non-detect.',[F,L,M])],
'pathways':[
('The studies establish that metals can remain in the edible fraction after ordinary washing and peeling. Nickel and lead were measured in prepared banana flesh in the Korean survey, and cadmium and chromium were measured in the dried edible fraction from Bahir Dar. A peel is therefore not evidence that the pulp contains no metal, and these results cannot be explained solely as contamination on discarded skin.',[L,M]),
('The papers discuss environmental and agricultural sources, including soil, water and farm inputs, but the admitted banana measurements do not pair individual farms, soil, irrigation water and edible fruit. They cannot apportion the measured concentrations between uptake during cultivation, deposition or later handling. For a supplier investigation, those pathways are hypotheses to test alongside edible-fruit measurements, rather than causes demonstrated by the market survey.',[L,M])],
'variation':[
('In the April–October 2019 Korean retail comparison, banana’s mean lead was 13.5 ± 15.0 µg/kg fresh weight versus 31.6 ± 29.0 in pineapple; banana was the lower observed mean of that pair. Nickel showed the reverse ordering: banana 107.2 ± 108.9 versus pineapple 86.6 ± 68.9. Chromium means were 24.7 ± 13.2 versus 22.4 ± 9.5, respectively. Each fruit had 15 samples. Ranges overlap, and the lead means include censored results; these are descriptive contrasts rather than demonstrated differences between all bananas and pineapples.',[L]),
('The Bahir Dar pools illustrate the same trade-off on a different basis. Banana had lower cadmium than orange, 130 ± 1 versus 147 ± 1 µg/kg dry weight, while chromium was higher, 773 ± 10 versus 759 ± 25. Forty fruits per type were collected and pooled; the paper reports triplicate analysis of triplicate digests (n=9). These small analytical SDs describe repeat measurements of pooled material; they do not establish how consistently individual sellers or farms differ.',[M]),
('These comparisons identify lower observed fruits within the stated study, not a lower-contamination banana cultivar or origin. The FDA collection market is the United States, not a verified growing country; the Korean table summarizes banana across its sampled market, and the Ethiopian result pools market fruit. Origin-linked independent banana lots, comparable edible preparation and adequate sensitivity would be needed to choose among growing regions. For lead, cadmium, nickel, total arsenic and chromium, no banana-origin or cultivar preference follows from these aggregate results; mercury and uranium have only the FDA non-detect bounds in the admitted set.',[F,L,M])],
'processing':[
('Washing and peeling define the edible material measured in the Korean and Ethiopian studies, but neither study reports paired banana concentrations before and after those steps. The measured residuals show what was present after preparation; they do not quantify a removal percentage, identify a peel-to-pulp partition or establish a washing recipe.',[L,M]),
('Mekonnen’s oven drying at 105 °C for 24 hours was analytical sample preparation before digestion. The reported dry-weight concentration is not a trial of banana-chip manufacture or a measured cooking reduction. Applying it to a puree, powder or baked food would require the actual water content, yield and any added ingredients, together with compatible input and output measurements. No numerical processing benefit is assigned from these surveys.',[M])],
'controls':[
('The source-supported control available from these surveys is monitoring the edible fruit. Mekonnen and colleagues recommend regular monitoring after measuring metals in the pooled fruit types; Lee and colleagues likewise call for continued targeted monitoring in their fruit assessment. This is a detection strategy and contextual recommendation, not a demonstrated percentage reduction in banana contamination.',[M,L]),
('For banana procurement, a representative sample of the material actually purchased and a method with useful reporting limits can inform a sourcing or acceptance decision. That application is an editorial inference from the measured variability and analytical bounds. Testing itself does not remove metal, and the studies do not supply a validated rejection threshold, sampling frequency or commercial treatment effect. The table retains the monitoring recommendation without presenting it as a removal experiment.',[F,L,M])],
 'tradeoffs':[
('Choosing a lower observed concentration for one analyte can move another in the opposite direction. Banana’s lower mean lead than pineapple in the Korean survey accompanies higher mean nickel; the Bahir Dar banana pool’s lower cadmium than orange accompanies higher chromium. A single-metal ordering therefore cannot justify an overall cleaner-fruit label, and none of these contrasts measures the effect of changing a person’s diet.',[L,M]),
('More sensitive analysis can also change the apparent picture without any change in the food. FDA’s all-non-detect lead result used a 4 µg/kg reporting limit, whereas the Korean table includes detected lead and a lower censored range endpoint. Different sample frames, years and reporting conventions accompany those methods. A count of detections is consequently useful only together with the concentrations, bounds and material; it is not an interchangeable rank of markets.',[F,L])],
'testing':[
('The FDA banana evidence consists of 27 prepared-food composites, repeated across seven analytes. Composite measurements average the material included in that sample; they do not reveal how many brands or independent lots were above or below a concentration. The Korean study instead reports 15 banana fruit samples with analytical triplicates, while Bahir Dar describes pooling 40 collected fruits per type, then triplicate analysis of triplicate digests (n=9). These are different units of observation and cannot be summed as if every laboratory result were an independent banana.',[F,L,M]),
('Reporting limits constrain the question a test can answer. FDA reported no banana cadmium detections at 1 µg/kg and no chromium detections at 50 µg/kg; the latter is a much less restrictive concentration bound. Lee’s Table 1 separately reports method detection and quantification limits, while Table 2 rounds its censored endpoints. For procurement, retaining the original result, limit and preparation is more informative than a bare “passed” or “not detected” label.',[F,L]),
('The admitted arsenic and chromium results are totals. Lee’s health model assumed all arsenic inorganic and all chromium trivalent, but those are model assumptions, not measured species. An inorganic-arsenic, methylmercury or chromium(VI) question requires the relevant analytical method; the elemental totals here must retain their original identities.',[L])],
'procurement':[
('The strongest sourcing information here is about the tested material and lot evidence, rather than a named cleanest banana origin. The Korean and Bahir Dar fruit comparisons reveal analyte trade-offs, but neither compares banana cultivars or independent banana lots grouped by growing region. FDA’s retail composites likewise do not identify cultivation country. A country-of-purchase label cannot fill that provenance gap.',[F,L,M]),
('A useful supplier comparison would keep edible fraction, moisture basis, analyte species, reporting limits and sampling unit aligned, then compare origin-linked banana lots over the relevant supply period. This is a proposed verification approach, not a study-tested schedule. A dried analytical result cannot establish a lower fresh-pulp supplier, and a low composite mean cannot guarantee every included lot. Rechecking the actual finished material becomes especially important when the purchase changes from fresh fruit to puree, powder or a mixed food.',[F,L,M])],
'product':[
('The data describe concentrations in sampled banana material, not individual health outcomes. The Korean paper combines fruit measurements with national consumption data and assumptions about species and body weight; the Ethiopian paper also models intake and risk from its dried-sample results. Those calculations do not measure absorbed dose or a clinical outcome in banana consumers, and they do not supply a personal safe-serving frequency.',[L,M]),
('Finished banana-containing foods need their own measurements and consumption context. None of the admitted raw-fruit rows identifies the concentration in a particular infant puree, cereal blend or pouch. Applying a banana mean to that product would miss its recipe, other ingredients and processing basis. This page therefore gives no infant-food safety guarantee or recommendation to replace one food with another from the fruit contrasts alone.',[])],
'assessment':[
('This is a partial assessment using a verified FDA dataset and two primary fruit surveys. It preserves source-specific numerical contrasts and censoring while leaving the remaining linked studies, banana-specific origin and cultivar comparisons, derivative extraction and intervention appraisal unfinished. The wider linked literature has not been declared absent or fully reviewed.',[F,L,M]),
('Current legal applicability remains a separate unresolved question. Study-author permissible-value comparisons have not been adopted here as current rules for fresh banana or infant puree. A legal determination requires the current primary instrument for the sales market, food form and analyte; these historical measurements do not establish lot compliance.',[])]}
