faqs=[
('ING-FAQ-01','What heavy metals have been found in banana?','FDA detected nickel and total arsenic in some raw-banana composites; lead, cadmium, chromium, total mercury and uranium were non-detect at the listed limits. The Korean survey measured lead, nickel and chromium in edible banana flesh. A study measuring an analyte does not mean it was detected in every sample.',[F,L]),
('ING-FAQ-03','Does this evidence show that organic bananas are cleaner?','No organic-versus-conventional banana comparison is established by these admitted datasets. Neither a collection market nor a low composite result is an organic-production comparison; a preference would need comparable labelled samples and actual analytical results.',[F,L,M]),
('ING-FAQ-04','Which banana varieties have the lowest measured metals?','The admitted tables do not split banana results into comparable cultivars. Their fruit-level summaries cannot rank Cavendish, red banana or plantain. A cultivar comparison needs independently sampled edible material on the same basis and analyte-specific results.',[L,M]),
('ING-FAQ-05','Which growing country has the lowest levels?','No growing-country winner can be selected from these aggregate results. US retail composites and Korean supermarket samples identify collection markets, while Bahir Dar pools market fruit. Those frames do not provide independent banana results grouped by verified growing country.',[F,L,M]),
('ING-FAQ-06','Can a raw-banana result be used for banana puree or powder?','It is not a measured product result. Puree, powder and mixed foods need their own preparation, moisture and recipe information, with finished-material measurements. The dry-weight Ethiopian result was made for laboratory analysis, not a commercial powder survey.',[M]),
('ING-FAQ-07','Do these concentrations establish whether a banana is safe?','No. A concentration in a study sample is not an individual exposure or clinical outcome. The papers’ risk calculations depend on intake, body weight and species assumptions, so they cannot guarantee an untested banana lot or establish a personal diagnosis.',[L,M]),
('ING-FAQ-08','Are the limits quoted in the papers current legal limits?','They have not been verified here as current rules for banana. A paper’s benchmark is not a substitute for the current primary instrument covering the sales market, food form and analyte. Current legal applicability remains unfinished in this assessment.',[]),
('ING-FAQ-09','How much metal does peeling or washing remove?','A banana-specific removal percentage is unresolved here. The Korean and Ethiopian surveys measured prepared edible portions but did not retain a matched unwashed or unpeeled banana baseline. Their residual concentrations cannot be used to calculate the effect of peeling or washing.',[L,M]),
('ING-FAQ-10','Is the arsenic result inorganic arsenic?','The reported banana arsenic results are total arsenic. Lee’s risk model assumed all arsenic inorganic; it did not measure an inorganic fraction. An inorganic-arsenic concentration must not be substituted for that total result.',[F,L]),
('ING-FAQ-11','Does the peel contain more metal than the pulp?','That ordering is not established by these edible-portion surveys. They separate or discard inedible parts before measurement and do not report a matched peel/pulp metal balance. Separate fraction measurements would be needed to answer it.',[L,M]),
('ING-FAQ-12','Did banana have less lead than pineapple?','Yes, the banana mean was lower in Lee’s sampled 2019 fresh-fruit comparison: 13.5 versus 31.6 µg/kg fresh weight, with 15 samples of each fruit. The distributions overlap and the lead means include censoring. Banana also had higher mean nickel, so this does not establish an overall safer alternative.',[L]),
('ING-FAQ-14','Does the study show that cooking banana reduces metals?','No paired banana cooking experiment is admitted here. Oven drying before digestion in the Ethiopian study was analytical preparation, not a household cooking trial. It cannot establish a cooking reduction or justify a detoxification claim.',[M]),
('ING-FAQ-16','Can these results set a safe number of bananas per week?','They do not establish a universal weekly allowance. The sampled concentrations and modelled intake assumptions are not personal measurements of total dietary exposure, body weight or absorbed dose. No serving frequency is calculated from these market averages.',[L,M]),
('EXTRA-ND','Does “not detected” mean zero metal?','No. It means the result fell below the stated method or reporting bound. FDA’s banana cadmium reporting limit was 1 µg/kg while chromium used 50 µg/kg. Those two non-detect results leave very different ranges of possible concentrations; the source’s zero codes are not measured zeros.',[F]),
('EXTRA-UNITS','Why are dry-weight banana results kept separate?','Mekonnen’s edible portion was oven-dried before analysis, whereas Lee reported fresh weight. Removing water changes the mass basis. Without a measured moisture conversion or preparation yield, a lower numerical result in one table cannot rank those materials or markets.',[L,M]),
('EXTRA-SAMPLE','Are repeated laboratory analyses separate bananas?','No. Lee’s analytical triplicates do not triple the 15 banana samples. Mekonnen describes pooling 40 fruits of each type, then triplicate analysis of triplicate digests (n=9); the resulting SD is not a measure of variability across 40 independent banana results.',[L,M])]
